1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,665
EV to EBITDA.

2
00:00:01,865 --> 00:00:02,961
Sounds scary.

3
00:00:03,161 --> 00:00:06,106
It is actually the fairest comparison tool we have.

4
00:00:06,306 --> 00:00:08,948
Think of buying a house that still has a loan on it.

5
00:00:09,698 --> 00:00:12,247
The price you pay the owner is like market cap:

6
00:00:12,247 --> 00:00:14,349
share price times number of shares.

7
00:00:14,549 --> 00:00:16,171
Say one thousand crore.

8
00:00:16,371 --> 00:00:19,871
But you also inherit the loan: four hundred crore of debt.

9
00:00:20,071 --> 00:00:22,389
And you get the cash sitting in the company's

10
00:00:22,389 --> 00:00:23,826
bank: one hundred crore.

11
00:00:24,026 --> 00:00:27,142
So the real cost of the whole business is one thousand,

12
00:00:27,142 --> 00:00:29,445
plus four hundred, minus one hundred.

13
00:00:29,645 --> 00:00:32,526
Enterprise value: one thousand three hundred crore.

14
00:00:33,276 --> 00:00:34,705
Now the earnings side.

15
00:00:34,905 --> 00:00:37,454
Start with operating profit from the P&L:

16
00:00:37,454 --> 00:00:39,407
one hundred and fifty crore.

17
00:00:39,607 --> 00:00:42,120
Add back depreciation and amortisation,

18
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:46,264
the paper charges for wear and tear on machines: fifty crore.

19
00:00:46,464 --> 00:00:49,547
That gives EBITDA, earnings before interest, tax,

20
00:00:49,547 --> 00:00:52,843
depreciation and amortisation: two hundred crore.

21
00:00:53,593 --> 00:00:57,282
EV over EBITDA is one thousand three hundred divided by

22
00:00:57,282 --> 00:00:59,780
two hundred: six and a half times.

23
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:01,965
The whole business, loans included,

24
00:01:01,965 --> 00:01:05,122
costs six and a half years of cash operating profit.

25
00:01:05,872 --> 00:01:06,866
Why bother?

26
00:01:07,066 --> 00:01:11,013
Because P/E gets distorted by debt and by tax rates.

27
00:01:11,213 --> 00:01:12,733
Two identical factories,

28
00:01:12,733 --> 00:01:17,443
one funded by loans and one by equity, show very different net profits.

29
00:01:17,643 --> 00:01:20,497
EV to EBITDA strips that out and compares the

30
00:01:20,497 --> 00:01:22,359
engine, not the financing.

31
00:01:23,109 --> 00:01:23,706
The catch:

32
00:01:23,706 --> 00:01:27,376
EBITDA ignores the money needed to replace those machines.

33
00:01:27,576 --> 00:01:30,833
A business that must keep spending heavily on equipment is

34
00:01:30,833 --> 00:01:33,060
worth less than its EBITDA suggests.

35
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:36,012
So always glance at capital expenditure too.

